[36m(PPOTrainer pid=723288)[0m INFO 03-03 11:28:05 ppo_trainer.py:441] [trace] step=45 reward=0.000 response_len=5379 total_len=9014 preview='system name=<|plugin|>\\nYou are an expert reasoner with extensive experience in all areas. You approach problems through systematic thinking and rigorous reasoning. Your response should reflect deep understanding and precise logical thinking, making your solution path and reasoning clear to others. Please put your thinking process within <think>...</think> tags.\\n\\nYour response should consist of a reasoning step (**thought**) followed immediately by a function call in valid JSON format. Wrap each function call using the `<|action_start|><|plugin|>` and `<|action_end|>` tags.\\n\\n**Format example:**\\n\\n```\\n(Your thought goes here...)\\n\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{\\n    "name": "tool_name",\\n    "parameters": {\\n        "parameter1": "value1",\\n        "parameter2": "value2"\\n    }\\n}\\n<|action_end|>\\n```\\n\\n# External Tools\\nYou have access to these tools:\\n[\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_molecular_weight",\\n      "description": "Return the average molecular weight (Daltons).",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_exact_molecular_weight",\\n      "description": "Return the monoisotopic exact molecular weight (Daltons).",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_heavy_atom_count",\\n      "description": "Return the number of non-hydrogen atoms (heavy atoms).",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_mol_logp",\\n      "description": "Return Wildman–Crippen cLogP (octanol/water).",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_tpsa",\\n      "description": "Return topological polar surface area TPSA (Å^2).",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_hbd",\\n      "description": "Return Lipinski H-bond donors count.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_hba",\\n      "description": "Return Lipinski H-bond acceptors count.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_num_rotatable_bonds",\\n      "description": "Return rotatable bonds count.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_fraction_csp3",\\n      "description": "Return FractionCSP3 = (# sp3 carbon atoms)/(# total carbon atoms), Often used as a simple “3D character / saturation” proxy from 2D structure.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_mol_mr",\\n      "description": "Return Wildman–Crippen molar refractivity (MR).",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_ring_count",\\n      "description": "Return total ring count.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_num_aromatic_rings",\\n      "description": "Return aromatic ring count.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_formal_charge",\\n      "description": "Return net formal charge (sum of atom formal charges as encoded in the SMILES).",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_qed",\\n      "description": "Return QED (Quantitative Estimate of Drug-likeness) as implemented in RDKit.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_num_heteroatoms",\\n      "description": "Return heteroatom count (non C/H).",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "describe_high_level_fg_fragments",\\n      "description": "Accurately parse functional groups with attachment points, corresponding fragment SMILES, and atom ids in the SMILES string for better structure description.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "The SMILES string to parse."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ]\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "remove_salts",\\n      "description": "Desalt a SMILES by removing salts/counterions and keeping ONLY the largest organic fragment. Use this tool when the SMILES contains multiple disconnected components separated by \'.\' and one or more components are likely inorganic ions/solvents (e.g., [Na+], Cl-, Br-, I-, [K+], [Li+], [Ca2+], [Mg2+], [NH4+], sulfate/phosphate, etc.), or when you want the parent (active) molecule for downstream property prediction.\\nIn aqueous/physiological conditions, salts typically dissociate; counterions are usually not part of the parent drug structure for many structure-based predictions.\\nTypical examples needing desalting: \'CC(=O)O.[Na+]\', \'[Na+].O=C(O)c1ccccc1\', \'CCO.Cl\'.\\nDo NOT use if the SMILES is already a single component (no \'.\') or if multiple organic fragments represent real mixture/co-crystal/combination therapy that should be kept. Output is the canonical SMILES of the retained parent fragment; smaller fragments are discarded.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "predict_pka",\\n      "description": "Predict pKa values for ionizable sites in a molecule. Returns base-site and acid-site pKa values (1-indexed atom map numbers), the most basic/acidic pKa, the number of basic/acidic sites, and the atom-mapped SMILES.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "estimate_logd",\\n      "description": "Estimate logD at a target pH from predicted pKa values and RDKit logP. Uses a Henderson-Hasselbalch approximation: logD(pH) ≈ logP + log10(f_neutral). logP is RDKit Wildman-Crippen logP. This is a heuristic intended for helpingyour reasoning and should not be treated as experimental logD. For polyprotic molecules, uses the most basic and most acidic predicted pKa values.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          },\\n          "ph": {\\n            "type": "number",\\n            "description": "Target pH (default: 7.4)."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "score_structural_alerts",\\n      "description": "Screen a molecule against RDKit\'s built-in structural alert catalogs (PAINS, Brenk, NIH, ZINC, ChEMBL, etc.).",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          },\\n          "alert_library": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "enum": [\\n              "all",\\n              "pains",\\n              "pains_a",\\n              "pains_b",\\n              "pains_c",\\n              "brenk",\\n              "nih",\\n              "zinc",\\n              "chembl",\\n              "chembl_bms",\\n              "chembl_lint",\\n              "chembl_mlsmr"\\n            ],\\n            "description": "Alert library to screen against (default: all)."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_labute_asa",\\n      "description": "Return Labute approximate surface area (ASA proxy) using RDKit rdMolDescriptors.CalcLabuteASA.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_fraction_aromatic_atoms",\\n      "description": "Return fraction of aromatic atoms (aromatic atoms / total atoms).",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_num_positive_charge_atoms",\\n      "description": "Return count of cationic centers (atoms with formal charge > 0).",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_num_negative_charge_atoms",\\n      "description": "Return count of anionic centers (atoms with formal charge < 0).",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  }\\n]\\nuser\\nInstructions: Answer the following question about drug properties.\\nContext: Membrane permeability is a measure of the ability of a drug to pass through a cellular membrane through passive diffusion, which is necessary for the drug to affect a cell. In general, small hydrophobic drugs can easily pass through membranes whereas large polar/charged drugs do not easily pass through. PAMPA (parallel artificial membrane permeability assay) is a commonly employed non-cell-based assay to evaluate drug permeability across the cellular membrane.\\nQuestion: Given a drug SMILES string, predict whether it\\n(A) is not permeable to cell membranes in a PAMPA assay (B) is permeable to cell membranes in a PAMPA assay\\nDrug SMILES: C1=CC=C(C=C1)CN2C3=CC=CC=C3C(=N2)C4=CC=C(O4)CO\\n\\nPlease think step by step and use tools when necessary (**Don\'t use the same tool more than once**). Then put your final choice ((A) or (B)) after "Answer:"\\nassistant\\n<think>\\nI need to analyze the drug\'s properties to predict its membrane permeability. The SMILES is: C1=CC=C(C=C1)CN2C3=CC=CC=C3C(=N2)C4=CC=C(O4)CO\\n\\nLet me parse this structure. It looks like it has a phenyl ring (C1=CC=C(C=C1) attached to a nitrogen (CN2...). There\'s a benzimidazole-like structure? Actually, N2C3=CC=CC=C3C(=N2) suggests a benzimidazole core. Then there\'s a furan ring (C4=CC=C(O4)CO) attached. So it\'s a relatively planar, aromatic compound with some polar groups (the CO group at end). Let me calculate relevant properties.\\n\\nFirst, I\'ll get molecular weight and other descriptors. Use get_molecular_weight.\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "get_molecular_weight", "parameters": {"smiles": "C1=CC=C(C=C1)CN2C3=CC=CC=C3C(=N2)C4=CC=C(O4)CO"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "Average molecular weight (Daltons): 304.3490", "function_name": "get_molecular_weight", "arguments": {"smiles": "C1=CC=C(C=C1)CN2C3=CC=CC=C3C(=N2)C4=CC=C(O4)CO"}}\\nassistant\\n\\n<think>\\nMolecular weight ~304 Da, which is moderate (under 500). Lipinski\'s rule of 5 suggests permeability likely if MW < 500. But permeability also depends on lipophilicity (logP), hydrogen bonding, polar surface area, etc.\\n\\nLet\'s get logP (Wildman-Crippen). Use get_mol_logp.\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "get_mol_logp", "parameters": {"smiles": "C1=CC=C(C=C1)CN2C3=CC=CC=C3C(=N2)C4=CC=C(O4)CO"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "cLogP estimate: 3.8369", "function_name": "get_mol_logp", "arguments": {"smiles": "C1=CC=C(C=C1)CN2C3=CC=CC=C3C(=N2)C4=CC=C(O4)CO"}}\\nassistant\\n\\n<think>\\nLogP ~3.84, which is moderately lipophilic (optimal for permeability maybe 1-5). That suggests good membrane permeability.\\n\\nNow topological polar surface area (TPSA). Use get_tpsa.\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "get_tpsa", "parameters": {"smiles": "C1=CC=C(C=C1)CN2C3=CC=CC=C3C(=N2)C4=CC=C(O4)CO"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "Topological Polar Surface Area (TPSA, \\u00c5^2): 51.1900", "function_name": "get_tpsa", "arguments": {"smiles": "C1=CC=C(C=C1)CN2C3=CC=CC=C3C(=N2)C4=CC=C(O4)CO"}}\\nassistant\\n\\n<think>\\nTPSA ~51 Å². Generally, compounds with TPSA < 60-70 Å² have good passive permeability. This is below that threshold.\\n\\nHydrogen bond donors and acceptors. Use get_hbd and get_hba.\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "get_hbd", "parameters": {"smiles": "C1=CC=C(C=C1)CN2C3=CC=CC=C3C(=N2)C4=CC=C(O4)CO"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "Number of H-bond donors: 1", "function_name": "get_hbd", "arguments": {"smiles": "C1=CC=C(C=C1)CN2C3=CC=CC=C3C(=N2)C4=CC=C(O4)CO"}}\\nassistant\\n\\n<think>\\nOne H-bond donor (likely the OH group). H-bond acceptors? Use get_hba.\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "get_hba", "parameters": {"smiles": "C1=CC=C(C=C1)CN2C3=CC=CC=C3C(=N2)C4=CC=C(O4)CO"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "Number of H-bond acceptors: 4", "function_name": "get_hba", "arguments": {"smiles": "C1=CC=C(C=C1)CN2C3=CC=CC=C3C(=N2)C4=CC=C(O4)CO"}}\\nassistant\\n\\n<think>\\n4 H-bond acceptors (nitrogens, oxygen). That\'s moderate.\\n\\nNumber of rotatable bonds: use get_num_rotatable_bonds.\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "get_num_rotatable_bonds", "parameters": {"smiles": "C1=CC=C(C=C1)CN2C3=CC=CC=C3C(=N2)C4=CC=C(O4)CO"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "Number of rotatable bonds: 4", "function_name": "get_num_rotatable_bonds", "arguments": {"smiles": "C1=CC=C(C=C1)CN2C3=CC=CC=C3C(=N2)C4=CC=C(O4)CO"}}\\nassistant\\n\\n<think>\\n4 rotatable bonds, not too high.\\n\\nNow, formal charge: use get_formal_charge.\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "get_formal_charge", "parameters": {"smiles": "C1=CC=C(C=C1)CN2C3=CC=CC=C3C(=N2)C4=CC=C(O4)CO"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "Net formal charge: 0", "function_name": "get_formal_charge", "arguments": {"smiles": "C1=CC=C(C=C1)CN2C3=CC=CC=C3C(=N2)C4=CC=C(O4)CO"}}\\nassistant\\n\\n<think>\\nNeutral molecule, good for passive diffusion.\\n\\nNow, fraction of aromatic atoms: use get_fraction_aromatic_atoms. This might indicate planarity and potential for stacking, but permeability is more about lipophilicity and size.\\n\\nLet\'s also get heavy atom count.\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "get_heavy_atom_count", "parameters": {"smiles": "C1=CC=C(C=C1)CN2C3=CC=CC=C3C(=N2)C4=CC=C(O4)CO"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "Number of heavy atoms: 23", "function_name": "get_heavy_atom_count", "arguments": {"smiles": "C1=CC=C(C=C1)CN2C3=CC=CC=C3C(=N2)C4=CC=C(O4)CO"}}\\nassistant\\n\\n<think>\\n23 heavy atoms.\\n\\nNow, consider typical permeability rules: For oral absorption, Lipinski\'s rule of 5: MW < 500, logP < 5, HBD ≤ 5, HBA ≤ 10. This molecule passes all. For permeability specifically, the "Rule of 5" is for oral absorption, but permeability across membranes is influenced by similar factors. Also, the "Egan rule" (for passive absorption) uses TPSA < 132 Å² and logP > -0.4. This molecule far exceeds that.\\n\\nPAMPA permeability often correlates with logP and TPSA. Compounds with high logP and low TPSA tend to be permeable. 